{"qid":"Q219817","names":["French Directory"],"title":"French Directory","endonym":{"native":"Directoire exécutif","lang":"fr"},"capsule":"The Directoire exécutif was the five-man executive council that governed France from 2 November 1795 to 9 November 1799, established by the Constitution of Year III as a bulwark against both royalist restoration and Jacobin revival. Its tenure was marked by persistent fiscal crisis, serial coup attempts (including the royalist Vendémiaire rising and the Fructidor coup of 1797), and spectacular military expansion under Bonaparte in Italy and Egypt. The Directory was overthrown on 18–19 Brumaire Year VIII (9–10 November 1799) when Napoleon Bonaparte, abetted by director Emmanuel-Joseph Sieyès, dissolved the legislative councils and replaced the executive with the Consulate.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is well attested; France's boundaries during this period are among the most thoroughly documented in early-modern European history.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Directory — French history","url":"https://www.britannica.com/topic/Directory-French-history"},{"label":"Wikipedia: French Directory","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Directory"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"French Directory","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Paul Barras","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":1795,"end":1799,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Jean-François Reubell","nativeName":"","roman":"","start":1795,"end":1799,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Louis Marie de 